**F1 — Holding pattern semantics provisional**
`HoldingPattern` is structurally minimal — no racetrack geometry, no entry procedure, no
timing. Needed for realistic IFR holds. Blocked on geometry work.
Impact: M | Effort: H

**RR-1 — ICAO 4444 broad baseline still too wide for blind downstream use**
The full-document normalizer now passes machine gate and the downstream baseline policy blocks
`manual_review_required` bundles plus `label_stub`/`table_fragment` structural debt, but the
mechanically eligible baseline still contains hundreds of bundles. Only the seeded trio
(`4.3.2.1.2`, `4.3.2.1.3`, `4.5.7.5.1`) should currently drive downstream consumers. We still
need staged rollout rules and more golden coverage before widening beyond that set.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-3 — Ollama-first transfer-family structure pass compresses nested enumerations** — DONE (2026-04-24)
The new Ollama-first requirement pipeline works on raw `ICAO 4444` slices, but the transfer-family
run over `4.3.2.1.1`-`4.3.2.1.3` still showed structural compression in the first pass: the model
flattened part of the arriving-aircraft substructure, effectively losing the separate `b)` branch
and blending nested list material into the `a)` item. Multi-attempt extraction plus reconciliation
helped candidate stability, but this structure defect still pushed later adjudication toward
`needs_bundle` / `needs_split`. Repeated structure attempts plus structure reconciliation now
preserve the nested transfer hierarchy in the transfer-family run. Remaining issues are tracked
under `RR-4`.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-4 — Ollama challenger sometimes ignores source modality when disputing authority** — DONE (2026-04-25)
Three-spike investigation in `run_icao4444_ollama_first_prototype.py`:
- Spike 1: directional rename `wrong_authority` → `authority_too_high` / `authority_too_low`. Surfaced
  cross-clause source contamination — the challenger pulled `should` text from §4.3.2.1.2 to argue
  about candidates sourced from §4.3.2.1.3.
- Spike 2: required `candidateSourceItemModalities` and `effectiveModality` fields, forcing the
  challenger to ground modality in the candidate's own `sourceItemIds`. Eliminated contamination;
  exposed three residual bugs (Notes-as-authoritative bias, direction inversion on advisories,
  structural concerns mis-labelled as authority verdicts).
- Spike 3: added Notes-as-non-normative challenger guard, verdict-direction sanity check, and a new
  bundle-gate stage between defense and judge. Bundle gate produces a mechanical `scopeComplete`
  per candidate; the judge now cites it directly in rationales instead of relitigating bundle/split.
Outcome: transfer family 2 → 3 accepted on the original Spike 3 run (readback 3 → 4); cross-clause
contamination, Notes bias, and direction inversion all eliminated and stable across reruns. The
v4 VMC/IMC judge inconsistency is **substantially reduced but not fully eliminated** — see `RR-7`:
a robustness rerun showed the bundle gate making asymmetric calls on parallel mutually-exclusive
sibling clauses (VMC scope=false / IMC scope=true on identical candidate shapes), demoting one of
the two transfer accepts to `needs_bundle`. Readback adjudication remains rock-stable across reruns.
Surviving challenger noise (mixed-modality structural concerns labelled `authority_too_high`) was
initially filtered by the bundle gate via the judge; closed as `RR-5`.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-5 — Challenger uses `authority_too_high` for mixed-modality structural concerns** — DONE (2026-04-25)
Spike 5 (prompt-only fix: reorder bundle gate before challenger; tell challenger to respect bundle
gate's `scopeComplete=true`) did NOT land — the model continued to misroute structural concerns
through authority verdicts on mixed-modality candidates regardless of prompt instruction. EGAST
regressed (3 → 1 accepted) from unrelated judge stochasticity (now tracked under `RR-8`).
Spike 6 fixed it deterministically: a Python post-step `apply_bundle_gate_override` examines the
challenger's verdict alongside the bundle-gate result and the candidate's classification, and
overrides `authority_too_high` / `authority_too_low` to `supported` only when:
  1. bundle gate `scopeComplete=true` on the candidate's items, AND
  2. candidate's `authorityClass` is consistent with its declared `modality`
     (per the modality-floor mapping `_AUTHORITY_CLASS_BY_FLOOR`).
The override declines when the candidate's authority is genuinely too high for its modality
(e.g. `should` clause classified as `authoritative_requirement`) — preserving real challenger
work. Audit records persist to `challenge_override/{candidateId}.json` for every fired override.
Outcome (Spike 6 across three families): transfer 3 accepted (matches Spike 3 best); readback 4
accepted (stable); EGAST 4 accepted — the best EGAST result yet, up from 1 in Spike 5 and 3 in
Spike 4. 9 of 10 raw authority verdicts overridden; the 1 decline (`req_comm_transfer_timing` —
should clause classified as authoritative_requirement) was correct: judge appropriately demoted
to `advisory_only`.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-6 — Ollama-first prototype generalises to EGAST advisory family** — DONE (2026-04-25)
The four-stage architecture (challenger → defender → bundle gate → judge) was tested on
`research/txt/egast-vfr-extracted.txt:539-608`, an EGAST VFR readback advisory family with
unnumbered prose, inline page-layout noise, orphan bullet markers, and a `best_practice`
authority ceiling instead of `authoritative_requirement`. The structure stage produced 14
reconciled items from 70 lines of layout-noisy source; the bundle gate sensibly returned
`scopeComplete=true` everywhere; the family ceiling held — no candidate promoted above
`best_practice`. Final outcome: 6 candidates, 3 accepted, 3 advisory_only.
Required mechanical adjustments: `num_predict` for extraction attempts and reconciler bumped
from 3500 to 8000, default `num_ctx` from 12288 to 16384, to absorb the wider extraction
output some attempts produce on advisory prose with bullet lists.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-7 — Bundle gate makes asymmetric calls on parallel mutually-exclusive sibling clauses** — DONE (2026-04-25)
Spike 7 deterministic post-step: `apply_sibling_symmetry_resolution` runs after all bundle-gate
calls have completed for a run. It groups candidates by their direct shared parent in the
structure (each cites a different child branch of the same parent), and if their `scopeComplete`
verdicts disagree it forces consistency by setting all to `scopeComplete=true` (the permissive
answer — each branch represents an independent operative scenario for its parent's rule).
Audits persist to `bundle_gate_sibling_resolution.json`. The bundle gate stage now precedes the
challenger and runs as a batch over all candidates so resolution can happen before the
challenger sees scope. The override declines to fire on non-sibling candidates and on
already-consistent groups. Spike 7 verification: same headline numbers as Spike 6 across
readback (4), transfer (3), and EGAST (4 of 6); resolution did not fire this run because
bundle gate happened to give scope=true on both VMC/IMC siblings, but the deterministic
resolution will activate the moment the asymmetric pattern recurs.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-8 — Judge invents over-conservative rules on best_practice content** — DONE (2026-04-25)
Spike 7 deterministic post-step: `apply_judge_conservatism_override` runs after the judge
returns. It overrides `decision=advisory_only` to `accepted` only when ALL of the following
hold: (a) candidate's `promotionHint=promote`, (b) post-override challenger verdict is
`supported` (or absent), and (c) bundle gate `scopeComplete=true`. Those three conditions
mean there is no basis in the inputs the judge was given for downgrading; the demotion can
only come from the judge inventing a stricter rule than its prompt actually contains.
Audits persist to `judge_override/{candidateId}.json`. The summary now annotates
judge-overridden rows. Spike 7 verification: EGAST `req_request_clarification` returned
`accepted` cleanly without override firing (judge happened to follow its own rules this
run); the deterministic guard will activate the moment the conservatism pattern recurs.
Impact: M | Effort: M

